<spell-notebook "emoji usage">
	<header level="1">
		Emoji
		<header>An emoji is a glyph used to represent something else.</>
	</>
	<emoji-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<html>
			<ui-menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
				<ui-item link="#using-it">Using it</>
				<ui-item link="#name-sets-and-loading">Name sets and loading</>
				<ui-item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
				<ui-item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			An emoji is the real Unicode character drawn by the reader's own emoji font, never an image: there is no image
			download and no per-emoji CSS, and it looks like the other emoji on their system.

			- `name` takes an emoji's CLDR shortcode, Unicode's own name for it (`thumbs_up`, `grinning_face_with_smiling_eyes`,
			  `flag_united_states`, `rocket`).
			- A name is case-blind, may keep its `:colons:`, and its words may be joined any way: `thumbs_up`, `thumbs up`,
			  `thumbs-up`, `thumbsUp` (handy from JS), `thumbsup`. An unknown name draws nothing.
			- These are the page's default NAME SET, `cldr`; Fomantic's names are another, and a page uses one set at a time.
			- No events: `link` is only a look.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Name sets and loading">
		<markdown>
			The names load in small pieces, one per first letter, the first time a name starting with that letter is used;
			later emoji from the same piece draw at once. `EmojiData`, exported with the element, picks the name set,
			preloads a name or adds your own.

			- `<ui-root emoji="fomantic">` switches its subtree to Fomantic's names (the shortcodes Discord and JoyPixels
			  use); emoji inside redraw when the root's `emoji` changes.
			- A switch from JS affects LATER lookups only: an emoji already drawn keeps its glyph, so switch before the first
			  emoji draws.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import { EmojiData } from "spell-ui/ui-emoji"

			EmojiData.use("fomantic") // later lookups use Fomantic's names;  "cldr" is the default
			EmojiData.register("approve", "👍") // <ui-emoji name="approve"> now draws 👍, in either set
			await EmojiData.get("tada") // loads the "t" names;  resolves to "🎉"
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			An emoji takes no focus and handles no keys, `link` or not: the `<button>` or `<a>` around it does.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- By default the emoji is plain text, so assistive technology reads the character's own Unicode name in the
			  reader's language ("grinning face with smiling eyes").
			- `label="..."` makes it a `role="img"` named by the label: use it when the emoji stands for something else, as
			  "Approved" does in the example.
			- An empty `label` marks it decorative (`aria-hidden`), for an emoji that only repeats nearby text. In a
			  framework, write `label=""`: some (MDX, JSX) turn a bare attribute into `label="true"`, which would become its
			  name.
			- An unknown name renders an empty box with no role, so nothing is announced.
			- `link` is only a look: the emoji takes no focus and does nothing on click. Put it inside a `<button>` or `<a>`
			  and give that control a name.
			- `loading` spins the glyph, and stands still under reduced motion; `disabled` only dims it.
			- If the emoji fails to render, the character and its name go: only children written inside the
			  element still show, through a bare `<slot>`.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
